.TH REPOQ 1 "2026-09-01" "repoq 0.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
repoq \- query the Repology API from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B repoq
.I project-name
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B repoq
\fB\-\-search\fR \fIsubstring\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B repoq
\fB\-\-outdated\fR [\fB\-\-search\fR \fIsubstring\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B repoq
is a lightweight command-line client for the Repology API
(\fIhttps://repology.org\fR). It shows which repositories package a given
project and at what version, or searches for projects by substring.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B repoq \fIproject-name\fR
Show packages for a single Repology project.
.TP
.B repoq \-\-search \fIsubstring\fR
Search for projects whose name contains \fIsubstring\fR.
.TP
.B repoq \-\-outdated
List outdated projects (can be combined with \fB\-\-search\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-repo\fR \fIname\fR
Only show packages from repository \fIname\fR. Only valid together with a
single project name.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-search\fR \fIsubstring\fR
Search projects by substring (batch mode).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-outdated\fR
Only show outdated/legacy projects (batch mode).
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
Limit the number of projects shown in batch mode (default: 200, which is
one API page).
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR
Print the raw JSON response instead of a table.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output. Shorthand for \fB\-\-color=never\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR[=\fIWHEN\fR]
Colorize output. \fIWHEN\fR is one of \fIalways\fR (the default, so output
survives being piped into tools like \fBbat\fR(1)), \fInever\fR, or
\fIauto\fR (color only when standard output is a terminal). A bare
\fB\-\-color\fR means \fIalways\fR.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-unique\fR
Collapse rows that share the same repo/version/status (hides sibling
sub-packages, e.g. "foo-bgpd" vs "foo-ospfd" from the same source
package).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show a help message and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Show version information, check GitHub for a newer release, and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
repoq firefox
.TP
repoq firefox \-\-repo aosc
.TP
repoq \-\-search fire \-\-limit 20
.TP
repoq \-\-search fire \-\-outdated
.TP
repoq firefox \-\-json | jq .
.TP
repoq firefox \-\-color=always | bat
.SH NOTES
A single repo can ship many sibling packages built from the same source
(e.g. "frr", "frr-bgpd", "frr-ospfd", ...), which show up as separate rows
with the same version/status; the PACKAGE column identifies which is
which, and \fB\-\-unique\fR can collapse them if you don't care.
.PP
For a single-project query, repoq also prints the version Repology tracks
as newest for that project and a link to its Repology project page, ahead
of the table.
.PP
repoq respects Repology's API terms of use: requests are throttled to at
most one per second, and a custom User-Agent identifying this tool and its
source repository is sent with every request.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success.
.TP
1
The project was not found, or a request to Repology (or, for
\fB\-\-version\fR, GitHub) failed.
.TP
2
Invalid command-line usage.
.SH SEE ALSO
Source and issue tracker: \fIhttps://github.com/pysio2007/repoq\fR
.SH AUTHOR
pysio
